{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"faq","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"is-tanstack-markdown-commonmark-compliant","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is TanStack Markdown CommonMark compliant?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. It continuously measures CommonMark behavior and preserves established matches, but full conformance is not the product goal. The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/conformance.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generated compatibility report"}]},{"type":"text","value":" records 403 of 652 CommonMark 0.31.2 examples matching after output normalization in v0.0.14."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"is-it-gfm-compliant","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is it GFM compliant?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. It implements the GFM features most relevant to documentation: tables, task lists, and strikethrough. Selected official GFM examples protect those behaviors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-it-render-mdx","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can it render MDX?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. It does not parse or execute JSX, JavaScript expressions, or module syntax. Comment components provide deterministic docs metadata without code evaluation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-i-use-it-for-user-generated-content","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can I use it for user-generated content?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raw HTML and executable URLs are disabled by default, but the library is not a complete sanitizer. Keep trusted boundaries disabled and apply a final sanitizer when your application’s threat model requires one."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-is-syntax-highlighting-not-included","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why is syntax highlighting not included?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlighters vary widely in bundle size, language coverage, theme model, and initialization cost. A callback keeps that choice outside the Markdown package and lets docs sites highlight at build time or on the server."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-not-add-every-commonmark-edge-case","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why not add every CommonMark edge case?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each rule adds parser code, interactions, tests, and maintenance cost. This project accepts syntax when real blog or docs content needs it and the bundle/complexity cost is justified. Compatibility percentage alone is not a reason to expand scope."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-i-transform-the-ast","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can I transform the AST?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. It is a public discriminated union made from plain objects. Transform it directly or use a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transformDocument"},{"type":"text","value":" extension. If the work needs a broad ecosystem of interoperable syntax trees and async plugins, unified is usually a better fit."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"can-i-parse-once-and-render-html-react-and-octane","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can I parse once and render HTML, React, and Octane?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. Every renderer accepts the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkdownDocument"},{"type":"text","value":". Core output structure is covered by React and Octane SSR parity tests."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"is-the-ast-stable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is the AST stable?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The documented AST is public and follows the version 1 compatibility policy. Existing valid documents remain accepted throughout 1.x. For persistent caches, record the producer package version and rebuild when you want updated parsing behavior. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./version-one.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Version 1 compatibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-do-i-decide-before-migrating","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do I decide before migrating?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review the "},{"type":"link","href":"../core-concepts/syntax-profile.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Syntax Profile"}]},{"type":"text","value":", run your content through the "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/testing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"downstream corpus test"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and inspect the generated output. Choose a broader parser when unsupported syntax is a content requirement rather than an incidental edge case."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"faq","text":"FAQ","level":1},{"id":"is-tanstack-markdown-commonmark-compliant","text":"Is TanStack Markdown CommonMark compliant?","level":2},{"id":"is-it-gfm-compliant","text":"Is it GFM compliant?","level":2},{"id":"can-it-render-mdx","text":"Can it render MDX?","level":2},{"id":"can-i-use-it-for-user-generated-content","text":"Can I use it for user-generated content?","level":2},{"id":"why-is-syntax-highlighting-not-included","text":"Why is syntax highlighting not included?","level":2},{"id":"why-not-add-every-commonmark-edge-case","text":"Why not add every CommonMark edge case?","level":2},{"id":"can-i-transform-the-ast","text":"Can I transform the AST?","level":2},{"id":"can-i-parse-once-and-render-html-react-and-octane","text":"Can I parse once and render HTML, React, and Octane?","level":2},{"id":"is-the-ast-stable","text":"Is the AST stable?","level":2},{"id":"how-do-i-decide-before-migrating","text":"How do I decide before migrating?","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# FAQ\n\n## Is TanStack Markdown CommonMark compliant?\n\nNo. It continuously measures CommonMark behavior and preserves established matches, but full conformance is not the product goal. The [generated compatibility report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/conformance.md) records 403 of 652 CommonMark 0.31.2 examples matching after output normalization in v0.0.14.\n\n## Is it GFM compliant?\n\nNo. It implements the GFM features most relevant to documentation: tables, task lists, and strikethrough. Selected official GFM examples protect those behaviors.\n\n## Can it render MDX?\n\nNo. It does not parse or execute JSX, JavaScript expressions, or module syntax. Comment components provide deterministic docs metadata without code evaluation.\n\n## Can I use it for user-generated content?\n\nRaw HTML and executable URLs are disabled by default, but the library is not a complete sanitizer. Keep trusted boundaries disabled and apply a final sanitizer when your application’s threat model requires one.\n\n## Why is syntax highlighting not included?\n\nHighlighters vary widely in bundle size, language coverage, theme model, and initialization cost. A callback keeps that choice outside the Markdown package and lets docs sites highlight at build time or on the server.\n\n## Why not add every CommonMark edge case?\n\nEach rule adds parser code, interactions, tests, and maintenance cost. This project accepts syntax when real blog or docs content needs it and the bundle/complexity cost is justified. Compatibility percentage alone is not a reason to expand scope.\n\n## Can I transform the AST?\n\nYes. It is a public discriminated union made from plain objects. Transform it directly or use a `transformDocument` extension. If the work needs a broad ecosystem of interoperable syntax trees and async plugins, unified is usually a better fit.\n\n## Can I parse once and render HTML, React, and Octane?\n\nYes. Every renderer accepts the same `MarkdownDocument`. Core output structure is covered by React and Octane SSR parity tests.\n\n## Is the AST stable?\n\nThe documented AST is public and follows the version 1 compatibility policy. Existing valid documents remain accepted throughout 1.x. For persistent caches, record the producer package version and rebuild when you want updated parsing behavior. See [Version 1 compatibility](./version-one.md).\n\n## How do I decide before migrating?\n\nReview the [Syntax Profile](../core-concepts/syntax-profile.md), run your content through the [downstream corpus test](../guides/testing.md), and inspect the generated output. Choose a broader parser when unsupported syntax is a content requirement rather than an incidental edge case.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: FAQ\n---\n\n# FAQ\n\n## Is TanStack Markdown CommonMark compliant?\n\nNo. It continuously measures CommonMark behavior and preserves established matches, but full conformance is not the product goal. The [generated compatibility report](https://github.com/TanStack/markdown/blob/main/reports/conformance.md) records 403 of 652 CommonMark 0.31.2 examples matching after output normalization in v0.0.14.\n\n## Is it GFM compliant?\n\nNo. It implements the GFM features most relevant to documentation: tables, task lists, and strikethrough. Selected official GFM examples protect those behaviors.\n\n## Can it render MDX?\n\nNo. It does not parse or execute JSX, JavaScript expressions, or module syntax. Comment components provide deterministic docs metadata without code evaluation.\n\n## Can I use it for user-generated content?\n\nRaw HTML and executable URLs are disabled by default, but the library is not a complete sanitizer. Keep trusted boundaries disabled and apply a final sanitizer when your application’s threat model requires one.\n\n## Why is syntax highlighting not included?\n\nHighlighters vary widely in bundle size, language coverage, theme model, and initialization cost. A callback keeps that choice outside the Markdown package and lets docs sites highlight at build time or on the server.\n\n## Why not add every CommonMark edge case?\n\nEach rule adds parser code, interactions, tests, and maintenance cost. This project accepts syntax when real blog or docs content needs it and the bundle/complexity cost is justified. Compatibility percentage alone is not a reason to expand scope.\n\n## Can I transform the AST?\n\nYes. It is a public discriminated union made from plain objects. Transform it directly or use a `transformDocument` extension. If the work needs a broad ecosystem of interoperable syntax trees and async plugins, unified is usually a better fit.\n\n## Can I parse once and render HTML, React, and Octane?\n\nYes. Every renderer accepts the same `MarkdownDocument`. Core output structure is covered by React and Octane SSR parity tests.\n\n## Is the AST stable?\n\nThe documented AST is public and follows the version 1 compatibility policy. Existing valid documents remain accepted throughout 1.x. For persistent caches, record the producer package version and rebuild when you want updated parsing behavior. See [Version 1 compatibility](./version-one.md).\n\n## How do I decide before migrating?\n\nReview the [Syntax Profile](../core-concepts/syntax-profile.md), run your content through the [downstream corpus test](../guides/testing.md), and inspect the generated output. Choose a broader parser when unsupported syntax is a content requirement rather than an incidental edge case.\n"}
